Graphify-Labs/graphify.body:
Found while auditing lesser-used language extractors for unreported bugs; no existing issue or PR covers this.

The Zig extractor's call resolver computes `is_member_call = "." in fn_text` for every call site, but never actually uses it: `tgt_nid = next((n["id"] for n in nodes if n["label"] in (f"{callee}()", f".{callee}()")), None)` accepts either the free-function label or the method label for every call regardless of how it was written. A bare call (`helper()`) can therefore resolve onto an unrelated struct's method of the same name when no free function by that name exists in the file, and a member call (`obj.helper()`) can just as easily resolve onto an unrelated free function when no method by that name exists on the receiver's type — whichever declaration happens to come first in the file's own node list wins, regardless of which one the call shape actually names.

Reproduced directly:
```zig
const Box = struct {
    pub fn helper(self: Box) i32 { return 1; }
};
fn helper() i32 { return 2; }
fn caller() i32 { return helper(); }
```
Before the fix, `caller()`'s bare call to `helper()` resolves to `Box.helper` (the method) instead of the free function - confirmed via the extractor's own output. The symmetric case (a member call landing on an unrelated free function) reproduces the same way with the call site swapped.

Fix: the already-computed `is_member_call` flag now picks which single label the lookup accepts - `.{callee}()` for a dotted call, `{callee}()` for a bare one - so a call can only ever bind the kind of declaration its own syntax names.

Added two regression tests, one per direction, each with a free function and an unrelated struct method sharing one name so the collision is unambiguous. Verified pre-fix behavior via `git show HEAD:<path>` + a temporary file swap (never `git stash`), confirmed byte-identical restoration afterward. Full suite: identical failure set to a freshly checked-out, unmodified `upstream/v8` (confirmed by running the exact same 44 test node IDs against a separate clean worktree - all 44 fail there too, none related to this change). `tools.skillgen --check` clean.
